1
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,140
in the river stay pretty much the same, and there's the same expectation for a

2
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:09,780
big one on the hook below. But what about the boy? Does he see himself as another

3
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:15,299
Huck Finn? Does he dream of steamboats and stern wheelers, or of river pirates just

4
00:01:15,299 --> 00:01:21,599
around the bend? He might. Who knows? But one thing is certain, he is aware of the

5
00:01:21,599 --> 00:01:28,060
changing world around him. Steamboats and stern wheelers, symbols of travel and

6
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:33,060
have been replaced by modern jet planes streaking across the sky.

7
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:40,060
And a stone's throw from his own backyard is a nuclear power plant.

8
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:47,060
Though he's only a boy, it's safe to say that his future is somehow related to jets and atoms.

9
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:55,060
How is it that this nuclear power plant is now a part of the rural horizon just beyond the small community of Elk River, Minnesota?

10
00:01:55,060 --> 00:02:01,060
A horizon which once boasted only barns, silos, and corn crims.

11
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:03,359
Let's go back a bit.

12
00:02:03,359 --> 00:02:09,259
In June of 1958, a contract was signed between the Elk River Rural Cooperative Power Association

13
00:02:09,259 --> 00:02:15,340
and the United States Atomic Energy Commission for the construction of a nuclear reactor.

14
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:22,300
A reactor plant that would add power potential to the existing RCPA generating plant, a plant

15
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:27,860
it was already producing 45,000 kilowatts of electricity to serve the expanding power

16
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:29,860
needs of the rural community of Elk River.

17
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:37,540
Thus, the reactor site at Elk River was chosen, a small farm community approximately 30 miles

18
00:02:37,540 --> 00:02:43,080
northwest of Minneapolis and St. Paul, and 800 feet from the Mississippi River, with

19
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:48,540
the reactor itself rising from a 40-foot sand knoll, symbolic of the power plants of the

20
00:02:48,539 --> 00:02:54,060
future, housing energies many times greater than those available from coal, oil, or gas.

21
00:02:55,739 --> 00:03:00,759
The Elk River reactor plant is part of the Power Demonstration Reactor Program of the

22
00:03:00,759 --> 00:03:05,680
United States Atomic Energy Commission, a program for establishing the economical and

23
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:11,519
technological feasibility of producing electrical power from nuclear energy, a program which

24
00:03:11,519 --> 00:03:15,599
will provide for the future power requirements of the Rural Cooperative Power Association.

25
00:03:18,539 --> 00:03:25,819
Building a reactor takes many months of planning and the efforts of many people,

26
00:03:26,159 --> 00:03:30,939
all benefiting from previous experience gained from the Atomic Energy Commission's program at

27
00:03:30,939 --> 00:03:37,479
plants in many parts of the country. All the materials must be specifically treated and

28
00:03:37,479 --> 00:03:43,259
inspected to meet AEC standards. Here the thermal shield which surrounds the reactor vessel is

29
00:03:43,259 --> 00:03:48,519
heated prior to the pouring of the lead shielding. This heating is done so that no air pockets will

30
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:56,080
form as the molten lead is poured in. The lead shielding forms a barrier to the gamma rays

31
00:03:56,080 --> 00:04:07,400
emitted by the nuclear reaction. When the pouring is complete and the lead is cooled,

32
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:12,879
the effectiveness of the shielding is tested by placing a radioactive source on the inside of

33
00:04:12,879 --> 00:04:18,120
thermal shield and a measuring device on the exterior as these are moved over the

34
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:23,620
surface the resulting densities are measured and recorded

35
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:36,000
the component parts are fabricated by subcontractors throughout the United

36
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,800
States and when they have been completed and tested they are brought to the

37
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:46,020
reactor site for final assembly. On the west coast, the reactor vessel starts its trip

38
00:04:46,020 --> 00:05:07,400
to Elk River by rail. The thermal shield arrives from the east.

39
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:27,080
of the various components begins.

40
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:31,740
As the reactor nears completion, the control rods are lowered into place while at the bottom

41
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:36,540
of the reactor, the drive assemblies which raise and lower the control rods are put into place.

42
00:05:55,240 --> 00:06:00,220
One of the final steps is the arrival of the fuel elements from their manufacturer.

43
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:15,820
At this stage the elements may be safely handled without special protection.

44
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:20,720
When the reactor is finally housed under the large domed containment vessel, there's little

45
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:25,500
evidence on the exterior of the energetic nuclear workshop within.

46
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:33,120
The containment vessel itself is a steel structure 115 feet high and 78 feet in diameter, lined

47
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:36,399
with a layer of concrete two feet thick.

48
00:06:36,399 --> 00:06:41,439
Completely enclosed, the containment vessel extends over eighteen feet into the ground

49
00:06:41,439 --> 00:06:46,300
and, as its name implies, is constructed to contain anything that occurs within the walls

50
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:48,360
of the vessel.

51
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:52,860
The heart of the nuclear power plant is the reactor vessel and core.

52
00:06:52,860 --> 00:06:57,220
This is where the controlled fission of nuclear reaction takes place.

53
00:06:57,220 --> 00:07:01,340
The tremendous energy of fission is released in the form of heat.

54
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:06,439
This is in reality no different from the heat generated in more conventional ways.

55
00:07:06,439 --> 00:07:11,699
As this heat boils the water, the water is transformed into steam and thus this type

56
00:07:11,699 --> 00:07:15,800
of nuclear reactor is known as a boiling water reactor.

57
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:21,300
Steam at 950 pounds per square inch pressure and 530 degrees Fahrenheit leaves the reactor

58
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:28,199
vessel and enters a steam generator or heat exchanger. This is primary steam. Secondary

59
00:07:28,199 --> 00:07:33,300
water entering the bottom of the steam generator is heated by the primary steam and is also

60
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:38,759
transformed into steam. Steam at a temperature of 506 degrees Fahrenheit and at a pressure

61
00:07:38,759 --> 00:07:45,300
of 715 pounds per square inch. This is secondary steam. The primary steam becomes water again

62
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:50,000
as it loses heat to produce the secondary steam and the water is returned to the reactor

63
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,060
vessel for reheating into steam again.

64
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:57,139
The secondary steam produced at the steam generator goes to a coal-fired superheater.

65
00:07:57,139 --> 00:08:01,920
Here it rises to 825 degrees Fahrenheit at 600 pounds pressure.

66
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:07,379
It then flows to the steam turbine, turning it at a high rate of speed, driving the electrical

67
00:08:07,379 --> 00:08:09,120
generator to produce electricity.

68
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:14,759
Thus, the continuous production of heat at the reactor core by nuclear fission results

69
00:08:14,759 --> 00:08:19,659
in primary steam to produce secondary steam which drives the steam turbine to

70
00:08:19,659 --> 00:08:24,300
produce electrical energy at the generator. In this manner the reactor

71
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:29,039
with a conventional superheater produces up to 22,000 kilowatts of electrical

72
00:08:29,039 --> 00:08:35,100
power on a continuous year-round basis. Only the primary steam is exposed to the

73
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:39,120
radiation of the nuclear reaction. The secondary steam which drives the turbine

74
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:43,740
does not come in physical contact with the primary steam at any time. In one

75
00:08:43,740 --> 00:08:48,000
sense this indirect cycle of the reactor plant system is like the hot air system

76
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,180
for heating a home the heat and gases from the fuel do not physically mix with

77
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:56,639
the air that is heated for discharge through the registers this room air is

78
00:08:56,639 --> 00:09:01,060
heated indirectly by the hot gases in the furnace and these hot gases then go

79
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:05,539
up the chimney and as a house has a thermostat to control the heating so

80
00:09:05,539 --> 00:09:08,680
must a nuclear power plant have a control for the amount of heat generated

81
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:13,760
generated. A nuclear reactor is a device in which the fission chain reaction of

82
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:18,220
atomic fuel is self-sustained. Control of this reaction is made possible by

83
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:22,720
positioning control rods of borated stainless steel relative to the core of

84
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:27,760
the reactor. Boron has the property of absorbing neutrons. Therefore as the

85
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:32,440
control rods are moved into the core the reaction is slowed down and conversely

86
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:39,440
as the control rods are moved out of the core, the reaction is intensified. In the

87
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:44,800
Elk River reactor, there are 13 of these control rods, each automatically moved by

88
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:49,580
individual control rod drives. These control rods are positioned to maintain

89
00:09:49,580 --> 00:09:55,760
a constant pressure of the steam at the inlet to the turbine. For example, if the

90
00:09:55,760 --> 00:10:00,580
turbine steam pressure drops, more steam is required. This means more heat must be

91
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:02,560
be generated in the reactor.

92
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:11,200
This is done by withdrawing a control rod slightly to increase the reaction within the core to produce more heat.

93
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:14,259
The reactor core is the heart of the nuclear power plant.

94
00:10:14,259 --> 00:10:17,560
The control room is the brain and the nervous system.

95
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:23,020
A multitude of gauges and recording instruments continuously sense the operation of the reactor

96
00:10:23,020 --> 00:10:26,400
and initiate any corrective action that is necessary.

97
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:34,139
Skilled and specially trained operators observe the operation of the plant as revealed in the control room and are alert to any variation

98
00:10:34,139 --> 00:10:38,659
in the operation that is not immediately corrected by the automatic control system.

99
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:45,139
If, for example, the shield cooling should become too low, the operator is warned and

100
00:10:45,139 --> 00:10:49,320
can take corrective action far in advance of any potentially dangerous situation.

101
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:56,799
And in an ultimate situation of danger, the operator can cause all the rods to scram or

102
00:10:56,800 --> 00:11:01,200
drop immediately into the core, thus completely stopping the nuclear reaction.

103
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:06,720
This gravity operated mechanical action of the control rods can be backed up with a flood of

104
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:13,280
boric acid which positively snuffs out the nuclear reaction. In normal operation with a plant

105
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:19,040
producing up to 22,000 kilowatts of electricity, the reactor will be shut down only for fuel

106
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:25,520
reloading or repositioning of fuel elements. Fuel handling during the removal of spent fuel

107
00:11:25,519 --> 00:11:30,639
elements is carried out completely underwater. The fuel elements are brought

108
00:11:30,639 --> 00:11:35,360
from the reaction cavity to the storage well through a 15-foot canal. There is

109
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:39,039
always eight feet of water over the fuel elements as they're moved from the

110
00:11:39,039 --> 00:11:45,379
cavity to the well. Racks are provided around the face of the storage well for

111
00:11:45,379 --> 00:11:51,139
storing 148 elements from a full core as well as room for an additional 20 spare

112
00:11:51,139 --> 00:11:56,840
elements for a total of 168 elements. Underwater lighting aids the operators

113
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:02,600
during the fuel element transfer operation. The 148 fuel elements are

114
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:07,139
arranged in the core in a complex grid pattern. Spaced portions between the

115
00:12:07,139 --> 00:12:13,100
various elements allow for the movement of the 13 control rods. Each fuel element

116
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:19,419
consists of 25 stainless steel pins, each 5 feet long. Each fuel pin contains the

117
00:12:19,419 --> 00:12:27,059
nuclear fuel pellets of thoria-urania, 120 pellets to the pin. Thus, for 148 fuel elements,

118
00:12:27,459 --> 00:12:34,779
there are 440,000 thoria-urania fuel pellets. The individual fuel pellets are uranium-enriched

119
00:12:34,779 --> 00:12:41,139
and are four-tenths of an inch in diameter and a half-inch in length. The amount of U-235 in

120
00:12:41,139 --> 00:12:47,819
the reactor totals 175 kilograms, which, if it were all consumed, would be equivalent to

121
00:12:47,820 --> 00:12:53,760
540,000 tons of coal. When operating personnel enter the containment vessel

122
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:59,100
for routine inspections, they enter through an airlock. This airlock prevents

123
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:02,980
any leakage of the containment vessel air during entrance and exit from the

124
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:07,320
vessel. There is an eight by ten foot door for freight and a two and one half

125
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:12,040
foot emergency door also with its own airlock.

126
00:13:12,039 --> 00:13:22,000
An air conditioning system will pass 3,000 cubic feet of filtered air per minute continuously

127
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,099
through the containment vessel.

128
00:13:24,099 --> 00:13:28,799
The filtered air is discharged through an exhaust duct located in the dome of the containment

129
00:13:28,799 --> 00:13:30,539
vessel.

130
00:13:30,539 --> 00:13:35,439
An air monitor at the inlet of the exhaust duct gives an alarm and closes the intake

131
00:13:35,439 --> 00:13:41,099
and exhaust ducts if radioactivity is present in the containment vessel air, thus isolating

132
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:43,960
the vessel from the outside air.

133
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:48,720
The Elk River reactor is constructed so that only an extremely small amount of radioactive

134
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:50,700
material will accumulate.

135
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:55,480
This amount is stored in shielded containers for later disposal in batches at locations

136
00:13:55,480 --> 00:14:01,300
on land or at sea, as specified by the Atomic Energy Commission.

137
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:05,759
Water will be discharged into the building drain system only when prior radiochemical

138
00:14:05,759 --> 00:14:10,519
analysis shows that the concentration of radioactivity is well within accepted

139
00:14:10,519 --> 00:14:16,279
AEC tolerances. Such water is kept in the retention tanks and recycled until

140
00:14:16,279 --> 00:14:22,919
measurements show it is safe for discharge. Thus the Elk River nuclear

141
00:14:22,919 --> 00:14:27,080
reactor operates within a sealed container, exposed only to its own

142
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:32,759
environment with no direct unmonitored contact with the outside. At a signal

143
00:14:32,759 --> 00:14:38,200
from the control room, the heart of the reactor starts to beat, and the power of nuclear energy

144
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:43,759
creates electricity, electricity traveling over high tension lines to the surrounding community.

145
00:14:45,379 --> 00:14:50,779
And as the Atomic Energy Commission's program continues, the shape of the nuclear reactor

146
00:14:50,779 --> 00:14:56,299
plant will become a familiar landmark on the American scene, utilizing atomic energy for

147
00:14:56,300 --> 00:15:01,340
electrical power that was untapped less than a decade ago.

148
00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:04,860
The Mississippi River may be oblivious to the changing age,

149
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:07,020
the age of atomic power.

150
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:09,400
But the boy cannot ignore it.

151
00:15:09,400 --> 00:15:11,280
He'll be a part of it.
